[Music]  nba legend bill russell is getting ready  to auction off some of basketball's most  valuable pieces of memorabilia later  this month they're all part of the 87  year old's personal collection including  championship rings most valuable player  trophies and game worn jerseys is being  put together by hunt auctions which much  of much of the proceeds going to his  charity mentor in a rare television  interview with the 11 time nba champion  we met on court in boston he reflects on  his life  his career and the obstacles he was  forced to overcome not just as a player  but as a black man in america  [Music]  basketball hadn't seen anything like  bill russell before  built like a skyscraper at six foot ten  when russell jumped russell soared  the kid from monroe louisiana who nobody  wanted on their team because of the  color of his skin he redefined what the  sport could be and eventually ended up  being the teammate no one could play  without bill this must been a great win  for you exactly  russell catapulted the celtics in the  city of boston skyward in the 1950s and  60s winning titles and breaking barriers  at a time when keeping your dignity as a  black man in america was easier said  than done so we're sitting here in  boston  at the garden  you helped bring glory here  11 championships here what does it feel  like to be here  on this court  i never was  comfortable plan for the celtics because  the number one paper was the boston  herald and they didn't like the idea  of the nba team having black players you  brought championships here in the time  when it was hard to be a black man was  it difficult  to be celebrated on the court  but then off of it not treated equal  first of all  was never separated on the court when i  got there was  the star was bob cousy  watch boston's bob cruisey no matter  what i did cause it was a star bill  russell another celtic taps it in i  remember i had a game where i had 25  points 25 rebounds 10 blocks and  boston harold said i was lucky i got to  play with cozy was it tough to play  in this city was it was it easy to win  over the fans i never bothered with them  fans because when i was a star the fans  called him the boston globies  like the boston globetrotters yeah  because you guys had black players yeah  i'm not a basketball player i am a man  who plays basketball bill russell the  man is someone who stood up for the  rights and dignity of all men  bill russell wasn't just an icon for  basketball fans to rally behind on the  floor of the boston garden on any given  night nine top negro athletes meet with  cassius clay he was an icon for black  america using his profile to highlight  racism and inequality  here with muhammad ali and other top  black athletes in 1967 backing ali's  decision refusing the vietnam war draft  at the march alongside dr king in  washington all while resetting the  standard of what was possible the boston  celtics were the first team to start  five black players and i believe they  got some backlash for that and our back  said  we just put the guys on the court that  were going to help us win and that's how  he looked at basketball well he got back  had a problem in boston that was  overwhelmingly  anti-black  they considered our back a traitor  because he coached black players yeah  see we were the first team to start five  black players do you remember in  kentucky where you and your teammates  set out because they refused  service at a restaurant  yeah  at that time why did you feel like it  was important to do that well things are  part of our  preparations for the game the team would  have  dinner together  and i had a couple of teammates from  kentucky  and they were going to have a separate  pregame meal  so i told the coach i'm not going to  play tonight he said why not  i said cause of that  later that month this letter found its  way to russell's mailbox it was from  jackie robinson who broke major league  baseball's color barrier more than a  decade earlier thanking russell for  boycotting the game in kentucky jackie  was of much more to him than most people  knew  they just knew he was the first black  player in the major leagues  jackie would not take anything off  anybody that's right  and so we're gonna play uh  the knicks  and he says uh  i'm to tell you guys i probably am of  you  jackie was uh not only tough  but he's probably the smartest guy to  ever play baseball i tried to get as  many as him i couldn't get there  which championship was the most special  to you was it the first one  or was it the last one the first year i  was player coach and we won a  championship first black coach in the  nba  is that right yeah  and you won as a player and a coach yeah  well you gotta understand something my  motivation was to make myself acceptable  to my folks i never felt like i was  making the contribution  i was just doing the best i could  the best he could wow one of the  greatest you know i wanted to talk about  what he did on the court and he kept  going back to what he accomplished off  of the court we love bill russell  because of his talent but i think we  need to admire him because of how he  handled  being a player  but being a black man at that time just  think about that dynamic  he was looked at as superhuman off the  court and less than human off of it  it's what you highlighted in this piece  nate and that it sort of dovetails with  what bill russell told you which is that  the game of basketball is a great game  but what you do with that platform for  your people and how you want to be seen  by the people is just as important if  not more and he and jackie robinson did  it well yeah so many others that came  after them all these young players yeah  guys like bill russell how do you want  to be remembered that's right when  you're done playing that's right  excellent what a wonderful piece we'll  be right back

